course: dictionary
lesson: 1131
title: Breathe: one word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To breathe means to take air into your body and let it out again. Breathe in takes air in, and breathe out lets it out. The words next to breathe are clues that show which way the air moves.
explain: «Before a vaccination you can tell the nurse you are scared, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand.» The word out tells you the air leaves your body, and slowly tells you to do it gently. The context shows you what to do.
explain: Breathe can also mean to say something very softly. In «She breathed a word», the word tells you she spoke, but so quietly it was almost like air. Check by swapping in your meaning: "She said a word very softly" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Breathe is a verb: breathe, breathes, breathed, breathing.
fact: «Breathe in» means take air in; «breathe out» means let air out.
fact: «Breathed-out air» contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.
fact: A hedgehog asleep in winter may breathe only a few times a minute, because its whole body slows down to save energy.
fact: Tadpoles breathe with gills at first, and later grow lungs.
exercise: «She took a deep breath before she jumped.» What does take a deep breath mean? | *Fill your lungs with a large amount of air | Close your eyes tightly | Stop breathing for ever | Shout very loudly
exercise: In «breathe out slowly», which word tells you where the air goes? | hold | slowly | *out | breathe
exercise: Tadpoles breathe with gills. What do fish use to breathe? | *gills | nose | skin | lungs
test: «She breathed a word of thanks before she left.» In this sentence, what does breathe mean? | To shout loudly | *To say something very softly | To take air in | To run very fast
test: A hedgehog in winter may breathe only a few times a minute. Why? | *Its body has slowed right down to save energy | It has a cold | It is playing a game | It is in a hurry
exam: «Breathed-out air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.» What is true of breathed-out air? | It has more oxygen than breathed-in air | *It has more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air | It has no air in it at all | It is always cold
